
---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Flowers & Perennials | Silken Valley Lifestyle"
  description="Explore flowering perennials, annuals, biennials, bulbs, roses, flower borders, and seasonal planting inspiration."
  heroTitle="Flowers & Perennials"
  heroText="Discover the beauty, colour, and variety of flowering plants."
  heroCompact={true}
>
  <section class="hero flower-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Flowers, Colour & Garden Inspiration</p>
    <h1>The Wonderful World of Flowers</h1>

    <p>
      Flowers bring colour, fragrance, texture, and seasonal beauty
      to the garden. From delicate spring bulbs and traditional
      cottage-garden perennials to spectacular summer borders and
      flowering shrubs, plants offer endless possibilities for
      creating beautiful outdoor spaces.
    </p>

    <p>
      Understanding the different types of flowering plants,
      their growing requirements, and their flowering seasons
      makes it possible to design gardens that remain interesting
      and colourful throughout the year.
    </p>
  </section>

  <nav class="flower-nav" aria-label="Flowers and perennials sections">
    <a href="#perennials">Perennials</a>
    <a href="#annuals">Annuals & Biennials</a>
    <a href="#bulbs">Flowering Bulbs</a>
    <a href="#roses">Roses</a>
    <a href="#borders">Flower Borders</a>
    <a href="#okanagan">Okanagan Gardening</a>
    <a href="#calendar">Flowering Calendar</a>
  </nav>

  <section class="flower-section" id="perennials">
    <p class="eyebrow">Returning Year After Year</p>
    <h2>Herbaceous Perennials</h2>

    <p>
      Herbaceous perennials are plants that live for several years.
      Their foliage and stems generally die back during the dormant
      season, while their roots, crowns, or underground structures
      survive to produce new growth when conditions become favourable.
    </p>

    <p>
      Perennials form the foundation of many traditional flower
      gardens. They can be combined with shrubs, ornamental grasses,
      bulbs, and annuals to create layers of colour and texture.
    </p>

    <div class="flower-grid">
      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Delphiniums</h3>
        <p>
          Tall flowering perennials known for their impressive
          vertical flower spikes, often in shades of blue, violet,
          pink, and white. They make striking plants for the back
          of a flower border.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Peonies</h3>
        <p>
          Long-lived garden favourites with large, fragrant flowers
          and attractive foliage. Peonies bring elegance to early
          summer borders and traditional gardens.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Iris</h3>
        <p>
          Elegant flowers with distinctive forms and a wide range
          of colours. Bearded iris and other garden iris varieties
          provide beautiful seasonal displays.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Daylilies</h3>
        <p>
          Adaptable perennials producing colourful trumpet-shaped
          flowers. Their variety of heights and colours makes them
          useful for mixed borders and informal plantings.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Rudbeckia</h3>
        <p>
          Bright, daisy-like flowers with golden petals and dark
          centres. These cheerful plants provide summer and
          autumn colour and attract pollinators.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Salvia</h3>
        <p>
          Aromatic flowering perennials with upright flower spikes.
          Many varieties offer excellent colour, pollinator value,
          and useful drought tolerance once established.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Phlox</h3>
        <p>
          Garden phlox produces generous clusters of flowers in
          shades of pink, purple, white, and red. It is a traditional
          choice for colourful summer borders.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Lavender</h3>
        <p>
          Valued for its aromatic foliage, purple flower spikes,
          and Mediterranean character. Lavender is particularly
          useful in sunny, well-drained garden locations.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="flower-section" id="annuals">
    <p class="eyebrow">Seasonal Colour & Flower Displays</p>
    <h2>Annuals & Biennials</h2>

    <p>
      Annuals complete their life cycle in one growing season,
      growing from seed, producing flowers and seeds, and then
      dying. They are especially useful for introducing seasonal
      colour into flower beds, containers, and garden borders.
    </p>

    <p>
      Biennials generally complete their life cycle over two
      growing seasons. They typically establish foliage during
      the first year before flowering and producing seeds in
      the second.
    </p>

    <div class="flower-grid">
      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Sweet Peas</h3>
        <p>
          Fragrant climbing annuals with delicate flowers.
          They are lovely grown on trellises, obelisks, and
          traditional garden supports.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Petunias</h3>
        <p>
          Popular annuals with abundant trumpet-shaped flowers.
          They are well suited to containers, hanging baskets,
          and colourful summer displays.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Zinnias</h3>
        <p>
          Bright, long-flowering annuals available in many colours
          and flower forms. They are excellent for summer borders
          and cutting gardens.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Marigolds</h3>
        <p>
          Cheerful annuals with golden, orange, and red flowers.
          They provide reliable seasonal colour in sunny beds
          and containers.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Foxgloves</h3>
        <p>
          Traditional cottage-garden plants with tall flower
          spikes. Many are biennial or short-lived perennials
          and are valued for their architectural presence.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Sweet William</h3>
        <p>
          A traditional biennial or short-lived perennial
          producing clusters of colourful flowers, often
          fragrant, for cottage-style planting.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="flower-section" id="bulbs">
    <p class="eyebrow">A Celebration of Seasonal Flowers</p>
    <h2>Flowering Bulbs</h2>

    <p>
      Flowering bulbs bring some of the earliest colour to the
      garden and provide spectacular seasonal displays. Many
      spring-flowering bulbs are planted in autumn, allowing
      them to establish roots before winter and flower the
      following spring.
    </p>

    <p>
      Bulbs can be planted in formal arrangements, naturalized
      beneath trees, grouped through lawns, or combined with
      perennials in mixed borders.
    </p>

    <div class="flower-grid">
      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Tulips</h3>
        <p>
          Classic spring bulbs available in an extraordinary
          range of colours, shapes, and flowering periods.
          They are beautiful planted in groups and coordinated
          colour schemes.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Daffodils</h3>
        <p>
          Cheerful spring-flowering bulbs with trumpet or
          cup-shaped flowers. They are excellent for naturalizing
          in garden borders and beneath deciduous trees.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Crocuses</h3>
        <p>
          Among the earliest flowers of spring, crocuses provide
          delicate colour in lawns, rock gardens, and planting
          beds.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Hyacinths</h3>
        <p>
          Fragrant spring bulbs producing dense flower spikes
          in shades of pink, blue, purple, white, and yellow.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Lilies</h3>
        <p>
          Elegant summer-flowering bulbs with striking flowers.
          Lilies offer height, fragrance, and architectural
          interest in perennial borders.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Alliums</h3>
        <p>
          Ornamental onions with spherical flower heads that
          provide distinctive structure and visual interest
          in late spring and early summer.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="flower-section" id="roses">
    <p class="eyebrow">Timeless Garden Favourites</p>
    <h2>Roses & Flowering Shrubs</h2>

    <p>
      Roses have been cultivated for centuries and remain among
      the most celebrated flowering plants in garden design.
      Their fragrance, variety of flower forms, and range of
      colours make them suitable for formal rose gardens,
      cottage borders, climbing structures, and mixed landscapes.
    </p>

    <p>
      Flowering shrubs provide another important layer of garden
      planting. They contribute height, seasonal flowers, foliage,
      and structure while helping connect herbaceous borders
      with trees and larger landscape features.
    </p>

    <div class="flower-grid">
      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Hybrid Tea Roses</h3>
        <p>
          Known for their large, elegant flowers, often carried
          individually on long stems. They are popular for
          traditional rose beds and cutting gardens.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Floribunda Roses</h3>
        <p>
          Produce clusters of flowers and often provide
          generous, repeated displays of colour throughout
          the growing season.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Climbing Roses</h3>
        <p>
          Useful for covering arches, pergolas, fences, and
          walls. Their flowering stems bring height and romance
          to the garden.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Old Garden Roses</h3>
        <p>
          A diverse group of historic rose classes valued for
          their fragrance, distinctive flower forms, and
          historical significance.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Hydrangeas</h3>
        <p>
          Popular flowering shrubs with generous flower heads.
          Different species and cultivars vary in size,
          pruning requirements, and growing preferences.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Flowering Shrubs</h3>
        <p>
          Lilacs, mock oranges, spireas, and other flowering
          shrubs offer seasonal displays and lasting structure
          in the landscape.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="flower-section" id="borders">
    <p class="eyebrow">Combining Plants Beautifully</p>
    <h2>Designing Flower Borders</h2>

    <p>
      A successful flower border combines plants with different
      heights, flowering periods, foliage forms, and textures.
      The aim is to create a composition that feels balanced
      while providing interest as the seasons change.
    </p>

    <div class="flower-grid">
      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Height & Layering</h3>
        <p>
          Place taller plants toward the back of a traditional
          border and shorter varieties toward the front.
          Island beds can be arranged with taller plants near
          the centre.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Colour Schemes</h3>
        <p>
          Consider complementary colours, contrasting shades,
          and harmonious combinations. A limited palette can
          create a calm and cohesive garden.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Flowering Periods</h3>
        <p>
          Combine early, mid-season, and late-flowering plants
          to extend the period of interest and reduce gaps
          in the garden display.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flower-card">
        <h3>Foliage & Texture</h3>
        <p>
          Include plants with different leaf shapes, sizes,
          and textures. Attractive foliage helps maintain
          interest even when flowers are not in bloom.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="flower-section" id="okanagan">
    <p class="eyebrow">Regional Growing Conditions</p>
    <h2>Growing Flowers in the Okanagan</h2>

    <p>
      The Okanagan presents wonderful opportunities for growing
      a wide variety of flowering plants. However, hot, dry
      summers, varying soil conditions, and winter temperatures
      make thoughtful plant selection particularly important.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before choosing plants, consider the amount of sunlight,
      soil drainage, available water, mature plant size, and
      winter hardiness. Grouping plants with similar water
      requirements can make garden maintenance more manageable.
    </p>

    <ul class="flower-list">
      <li>Choose plants suited to local winter temperatures.</li>
      <li>Improve soil structure and drainage where necessary.</li>
      <li>Use mulch to help conserve soil moisture.</li>
      <li>Group plants according to their sunlight and watering needs.</li>
      <li>Consider drought-tolerant perennials for sunny locations.</li>
      <li>Provide appropriate irrigation during establishment.</li>
      <li>Allow sufficient room for mature plant growth.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="flower-section" id="calendar">
    <p class="eyebrow">Planning Seasonal Colour</p>
    <h2>A Seasonal Flowering Calendar</h2>

    <p>
      A carefully planned garden can offer flowers and foliage
      interest from early spring through autumn. Actual flowering
      periods depend on the plant variety, weather, and growing
      conditions.
    </p>

    <div class="season-grid">
      <article class="season-card">
        <h3>Spring</h3>
        <p class="season-plants">
          Crocuses, daffodils, tulips, hyacinths, and early iris.
        </p>
        <p>
          Welcome the growing season with spring bulbs and
          early-flowering perennials.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="season-card">
        <h3>Early Summer</h3>
        <p class="season-plants">
          Peonies, iris, delphiniums, roses, and salvia.
        </p>
        <p>
          Build generous flower displays with a combination
          of traditional perennials and flowering shrubs.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="season-card">
        <h3>Late Summer</h3>
        <p class="season-plants">
          Daylilies, phlox, rudbeckia, zinnias, and lavender.
        </p>
        <p>
          Extend colour through the warmest months with
          long-flowering plants and summer annuals.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="season-card">
        <h3>Autumn</h3>
        <p class="season-plants">
          Asters, sedum, chrysanthemums, and ornamental grasses.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finish the season with rich colours, seed heads,
          and late-flowering plants.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="flower-closing">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Beauty of a Flower Garden</p>
    <h2>Planting a Garden to Enjoy for Years</h2>
    <p>
      Whether creating a traditional perennial border,
      a romantic cottage garden, a colourful cutting garden,
      or a carefully planned landscape, flowers bring life
      and personality to every outdoor space.
    </p>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .flower-intro {
    max-width: 850px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .flower-intro > p:not(.eyebrow) {
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  .flower-nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: .65rem;
    margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .flower-nav a {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: .5rem .9rem;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 20px;
    color: var(--accent-dark);
    font-size: .9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .flower-nav a:hover {
    background: var(--accent);
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  .flower-section {
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 14px;
    box-shadow: 0 3px 12px rgba(90, 50, 65, .04);
    scroll-margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .flower-section h2,
  .flower-closing h2 {
    color: var(--accent-dark);
    margin-top: .25rem;
  }

  .flower-section > p:not(.eyebrow) {
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  .flower-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .flower-card {
    padding: 1.2rem;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
  }

  .flower-card h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    color: var(--accent-dark);
  }

  .flower-card p {
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 0;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .flower-list {
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.9;
  }

  .season-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .season-card {
    padding: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
  }

  .season-card h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    color: var(--accent-dark);
  }

  .season-card p {
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .season-card .season-plants {
    color: var(--accent-dark);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .flower-closing {
    margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    text-align: center;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }

  .flower-closing p:last-child {
    max-width: 700px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .flower-section {
      padding: 1.25rem;
    }

    .flower-grid,
    .season-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .flower-nav {
      gap: .5rem;
      padding: 1rem;
    }

    .flower-nav a {
      font-size: .85rem;
    }

    .flower-closing {
      padding: 1.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
